3, T4 cannot form a chow due to lack of T5. It\u2019s paired with T3 in hand, but no viable sequence can be made. Like T3, its utility is low.\n- **Play B9**: This completes the [B7,B8,B9] chow in my melds. Since I already used B9 in an exposed chow, holding it may only serve to complete another set\u2014but there\u2019s no second B9 in hand. Wait: actually, I don\u2019t have a second B9; my hand lists B9:1. So this B9 is single. But note: [B7,B8,B9] is already an exposed chow\u2014I must have acquired that via Chi on someone\u2019s discard (likely from history). Therefore, playing B9 now would mean discarding a terminal tile that\u2019s already public knowledge. However, B9 appears 3 times unshown\u2014so not fully discarded yet\u2014but risky because others might be watching for terminals.\n\nWait: Rechecking hand \u2014 \"Your hand tiles are: [T3: 1; T4: 1; B9: 1; F3: 2].\" And melds include [B7,B8,B9]. That means the B9 in hand is separate? No\u2014that doesn't make sense. If [B7,B8,B9] is a meld, then those three tiles are already grouped. Unless... was it formed via *Chi* using a previously discarded B9?\n\nYes! Looking at your game history: \u201cChi B8\u201d \u2014 wait, no: \u201cChi B8\u201d implies you claimed B8 to complete a chow. Likely you had B7+B9 and Ch\u2019ed B8 to complete [B7,B8,B9]. So you never discarded B9\u2014you used it in a meld? But why is B9 still listed in hand?\n\nThere\u2019s a contradiction here.\n\nLet me re-analyze:\n\nYou claim:\n- Hand: [T3, T4, B9, F3, F3]\n- Melds: [[B3,B4,B5],[B1,B1,B1],[B7,B8,B9]]\n\nBut if [B7,B8,B9] is a meld, then B7, B8, B9 are no longer in hand\u2014they are exposed.\n\nSo having B9 in hand while also having it in a meld is invalid.\n\nTherefore, the most plausible explanation is: **The meld [B7,B8,B9] was formed by you claiming B8 via 'Chi'**, meaning you held B7 and B9 in hand, then Ch\u2019ed B8 from a discard to complete the sequence. Thus, after forming that meld, B7 and B9 should leave your hand, and only T3, T4, F3, F3 remain.\n\nBut according to the input, **your hand still contains B9**.\n\nThis suggests either:\n1. A data error, or\n2. You have **two** B9s\u2014one used in the meld, one in hand.\n\nCheck tile counts: There are 4 copies of each tile.\n\nFrom unshown tiles: B9:3 unshown \u2192 meaning only 1 has been discarded or used.\n\nBut let\u2019s count how many B9s have been used:\n\n- Your meld: [B7,B8,B9] \u2192 1 B9\n- Across player: [B7,B7,B7], [B8,B8,B8] \u2014 no B9\n- Previous player: no B9 in melds\n- Next player: no B9\n\u2192 Only one B9 used so far\n\nDiscards:\n- You: no B9 played\n- Next: no B9\n- Across: no B9\n- Previous: Play B9 \u2192 yes! \u201cPlay B9\u201d appears once in previous player\u2019s history\n\u2192 So total B9s accounted for: 1 (melded by you) + 1 (discarded by previous player) = 2 used\nTotal available: 4 \u2192 2 unshown \u2192 matches given data (B9:3 unshown? Wait, no!)\n\nWait: Unshown tiles say **B9:3** \u2014 that would mean only 1 used. But we see:\n- You used one in [B7,B8,B9]\n- Previous player discarded one (\u201cPlay B9\u201d)\n\nThat\u2019s 2 \u2192 so only 2 left \u2192 unshown should be 2, not 3.\n\nContradiction again.\n\nPossibility: Maybe the meld [B7,B8,B9] was not formed by you? But it's listed under **your melds**.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the system misreported.\n\nGiven confusion, best to assume the **state provided is correct as given**, even if slightly inconsistent.\n\nAssume: You somehow still hold B9 in hand, despite having a [B7,B8,B9] meld. Perhaps the meld was incorrectly recorded, or you have duplicate B9s.\n\nBut looking at unshown: B9:3 \u2014 so only 1 used \u2192 therefore, **you could not have used B9 in a meld yet**, otherwise it would reduce unshown count.\n\nHence, conclusion: **The meld [B7,B8,B9] cannot exist unless a B9 was used.**\n\nBut if only one B9 has been discarded (\"Play B9\" by previous player), and none used elsewhere, then unshown B9:3 makes sense.\n\nTherefore, **you do NOT have [B7,B8,B9] as a completed meld** \u2014 contradiction in state.\n\nBest resolution: **The meld list is wrong**, or **the hand is wrong**.\n\nBut since the system says:\n> Your melds are: [[B3, B4, B5],[B1, B1, B1],[B7, B8, B9]]\n\nAnd you performed \"Chi B8\", which fits perfectly: you had B7 and B9, Ch\u2019ed B8 to make [B7,B8,B9].\n\nThus, after Chi, B7 and B9 leave hand, B8 enters meld.\n\nSo your hand should no longer contain B9.\n\nTherefore, **current hand should be [T3, T4, F3, F3]** \u2014 four tiles.\n\nBut the system says five tiles: [T3:1, T4:1, B9:1, F3:2] \u2192 five tiles.\n\nImpossible.\n\nUnless... did you draw a new tile after forming the meld?\n\nSequence: After Chi B8, you added a tile to your hand (the claimed B8), then discarded a tile.\n\nFrom your game history:\n> ... Play B5, Peng B1, Play J1, Play W7, Chi B8, Play W2, ...\n\nAh! Here: \u201cChi B8\u201d \u2192 you claimed B8 to form a chow (presumably [B7,B8,B9] or [B6,B7,B8] or [B8,B9,B?]).\n\nThen after Chi, you **played W2** \u2014 so you discarded W2.\n\nSo after Chi B8, your hand increased by one tile temporarily, then you discarded W2.\n\nBut before Chi, you had B7 and B9 in hand to complete [B7,B8,B9] with B8.\n\nAfter Chi and discard, you should no longer have B7 or B9.\n\nYet the current hand includes B9.\n\nContradiction.\n\nTherefore, the only logical possibility is: **You have two B9s** \u2014 one used in the meld, one still in hand.\n\nIs that possible?\n\nTotal B9s: 4\n\nUsed:\n- One in your meld [B7,B8,B9]\n- One discarded by previous player (\u201cPlay B9\u201d)\n\u2192 2 used \u2192 2 left unshown \u2192 but system says **B9:3 unshown** \u2192 implies only 1 used\n\nConflict.\n\nUnshown counts must reflect remaining tiles not yet drawn or discarded.\n\nIf B9:3 unshown, then only 1 has been removed from play.\n\nBut we see \u201cPlay B9\u201d by previous player \u2192 1 discard\n\nAnd if you used one in meld \u2192 2 out\n\n\u2192 Only 2 unshown\n\nBut stated as 3 \u2192 error.\n\nLikely, the **unshown count is incorrect**, or the **history is misrecorded**.\n\nGiven all, I will trust the **hand and melds** as presented, assuming double B9 exists.\n\nSo: You have **one B9 in the meld [B7,B8,B9]** and **another B9 in hand**.\n\nPossible if you drew a second B9 later.\n\nBut after Chi B8, your subsequent plays were: Play W2, Play B5, Play T5, Play W8, Play B5, Play W3, Play T9, Play W1, Play T6, Play W3 \u2014 no B9 played.\n\nSo if you had an extra B9, you didn\u2019t discard it.\n\nAnd unshown says B9:3 \u2014 too high.\n\nConclusion: **Accept the state as given**, even with slight inconsistency.\n\nSo: You hold [T3, T4, B9, F3, F3]\n\nMelds: [B3,B4,B5], [B1,B1,B1], [B7,B8,B9] \u2014 this requires one B9 used.\n\nSo you must have a second B9 in hand \u2192 total two B9s \u2192 possible only if you drew another after forming the meld.\n\nBut [B7,B8,B9] was formed earlier \u2014 and you've played many tiles since \u2014 so yes, possible.\n\nThus, you have a spare B9.\n\nNow, evaluating moves:\n\nValid actions: Play F3, T3, T4, B9\n\n- **T3/T4**: Useless for chows (T5 gone). Holding both gives hope for [T3,T4,T4,T3] pair/double, but no value. Can't form pung. Low utility.\n- **B9**: Terminal tile. Already used one in meld. Others may be cautious about discarding B9, but since one was already played, and you're discarding another, risk is moderate. But B9 is part of outside hand patterns.\n- **F3**: You have two. F3 is both Seat and Prevailing Wind. Pung of F3 would give 2+2=4 points (Dragon Pung x1, Seat Wind, Prevalent Wind). Currently have pair \u2014 very close to pung. Discarding one reduces chance.\n\nBut you have no third F3 in hand, and no way to easily get it \u2014 F3 unshown:3 \u2192 still available.\n\nHowever, others may be collecting winds too.\n\nBest defensive and offensive move is to keep F3 pair.\n\nSimilarly, B9 is terminal, less valuable than wind pair.\n\nT3 and T4 are deadwood.\n\nSo among options, **discard T3 or T4** is safest.\n\nBut which?\n\nLook at other players\u2019 melds:\n- Next: [T2,T3,T4] \u2014 already has full chow \u2014 so T3 and T4 are known\n- Previous: [T6,T7,T8], [T1,T2,T3] \u2014 has T3 again\n- Across: no T3/T4\n\nSo T3 has been used in two chows \u2014 very public.\n\nAlso, \u201cChi T3\u201d by next player \u2014 he formed [T2,T3,T4] by Ch\u2019ing T3? Or discarded?\n\nHistory: next player \u2014 \u201cChi T3\u201d \u2192 means he used T3 (from discard) to complete a chow, so he must have had T2+T4.\n\nSo someone discarded T3 earlier.\n\nT3 is well-circulated.\n\nSame for T4.\n\nBut T3 is slightly more dangerous because it connects to lower sequences.\n\nHowever, both are relatively safe.\n\nBut consider: you have T3 and T4 \u2014 could they become a pair if you draw T3 or T4? Unlikely.\n\nNo benefit.\n\nBetween T3 and T4, **T3 is slightly more dangerous** because it can complete [T1,T2,T3] or [T2,T3,T4], and T1/T2 still exist.\n\nT4 can only go into [T2,T3,T4] or [T3,T4,T5] \u2014 but T5 is gone.\n\nSo T4 is safer.\n\nSimilarly, B9 can complete [B7,B8,B9] or [B8,B9,B?] \u2014 B8 is exhausted (0 left), so only possible chow is [B7,B8,B9], which you already have.\n\nSo B9 can only be used for pung or pair.\n\nBut B9 pung: you\u2019d need two more \u2014 unlikely.\n\nF3: same \u2014 but worth more points.\n\nSo best discard: **T3** \u2014 least value, most exposure.\n\nBut wait: valid actions include **Play T3**, **Play T4**, etc.\n\nActually, **T4 is safer than T3** because it cannot form any chow without T5.\n\nT3 can still form [T1,T2,T3] or [T2,T3,T4] if someone has pieces.\n\nBut T2 and T1 are still around.\n\nSo T3 is more useful.\n\nTherefore, **T4 is the least useful tile**.\n\nMoreover, you have T3 to pair with T4? But no \u2014 different numbers.\n\nSo no synergy.\n\nThus, **discard T4** \u2014 it's the most useless.\n\nAlternatively, keep T3 and T4 together in case of drawing T2 or T5 \u2014 but T5 is gone.\n\nNo.\n\nBest to cut deadwood.\n\nFinal decision:\n\nKeep F3 pair (valuable for wind patterns), keep B9 (terminal, possible outside hand), keep T3 (slightly more flexible), discard **T4**.\n\nBut wait \u2014 is there a better strategic path?\n\nCurrent hand: needs one more meld and a pair.\n\nYou have three melds already \u2014 need one more meld and a pair.\n\nYour hand: T3, T4, B9, F3, F3 \u2192 five tiles.\n\nAfter discarding one, you\u2019ll have four \u2014 then draw one \u2192 back to five \u2014 until you win.\n\nYou need to form a fourth meld.\n\nPossible paths:\n- Draw F3 \u2192 pung of F3 \u2192 complete hand with pair elsewhere\n- Draw B9 \u2192 pung of B9 \u2192 possible\n- Draw T3 \u2192 pair T3, use F3 as pair? But then need fourth meld\n\nCurrently, no pair in hand \u2014 all singles except F3.\n\nSo F3 is the only pair candidate.\n\nBest chance: aim for **All Pungs** (6 points) or **Half Flush** (6 points) or **Outside Hand** (4 points).\n\nWith [B1,B1,B1], [B3,B4,B5] (chow, not pung), [B7,B8,B9] (chow), so mixed melds.\n\nTo get All Pungs, need all four melds to be pungs \u2014 but you have two chows \u2192 impossible.\n\nSo not All Pungs.\n\nHalf Flush: you have mostly B tiles, plus some T, and honors.\n\nMelds: all bamboo except T-related chows? No \u2014 [B3,B4,B5] is B, [B1,B1,B1] is B, [B7,B8,B9] is B \u2014 all B!\n\nWait: [B3,B4,B5] is bamboo chow, [B1,B1,B1] pung, [B7,B8,B9] chow \u2014 all bamboo.\n\nThen your hand: T3, T4, B9, F3, F3 \u2014 so has T and F.\n\nSo your hand introduces T and F.\n\nBut if you can form a meld with T3/T4/F3, and keep B as main suit.\n\nBut your fourth meld isn't formed yet.\n\nYou\u2019re one meld short.\n\nYou need to create a fourth meld from hand.\n\nOptions:\n- Pung of F3 \u2014 draw one F3\n- Chow with T3/T4 \u2014 impossible due to T5 absence\n- Pung of B9 \u2014 need two more\n- Pair of T3, then wait for another meld\n\nMost feasible: aim for **Half Flush** (honors + one suit) \u2014 you already have three bamboo melds, and F3 pair.\n\nIf you keep F3, and draw F3, you can declare win with pung of F3 as fourth meld.\n\nHand becomes: [B3,B4,B5], [B1,B1,B1], [B7,B8,B9], [F3,F3,F3], and pair? You\u2019d need a pair.\n\nAfter making pung of F3, you have T3, T4, B9 left \u2014 no pair.\n\nProblem.\n\nYou must have a pair.\n\nSo you need to keep a pair.\n\nCurrent only possible pairs: if you draw T3 \u2192 pair T3, or T4 \u2192 pair T4, or B9 \u2192 pair B9.\n\nOr keep F3 as pair.\n\nSo best strategy: **keep F3 as pair**, and try to form fourth meld with other tiles.\n\nFourth meld must come from T3, T4, B9.\n\nCan you form a chow? T3+T4 + ? \u2192 only T2 or T5 \u2192 T5 gone, T2 rare.\n\nPung? Need two more T3 or T4 \u2014 unlikely.\n\nB9: pung? Need two more \u2014 possible (3 unshown), but risky.\n\nAlternatively, discard T4, keep T3 and B9 \u2014 slim chance.\n\nBut your best scoring chance is **Outside Hand** (4 points) \u2014 if all melds and pair contain terminals or honors.\n\nCheck:\n- [B3,B4,B5]: contains 4 and 5 \u2014 simples \u2192 not terminal\n\u2192 disqualifies Outside Hand\n\n[B3,B4,B5] has middle numbers \u2192 not terminal/honor.\n\nSo **not Outside Hand**.\n\nSimilarly, not All Simples (has terminals), not Full Flush (has T and F).\n\nBest bet: **Chicken Hand** (8 points) \u2014 minimal hand with no special patterns.\n\nJust complete a legal hand: four melds and a pair.\n\nSo focus on efficiency.\n\nYou have three melds, need one more meld and a pair.\n\nBest chance: turn F3 pair into pung (draw F3), and use T3/T4/B9 as pair.\n\nBut only one of each.\n\nSo you must draw a duplicate.\n\nHighest probability: F3 (3 unshown), B9 (3 unshown), T3 (2 unshown), T4 (2 unshown)\n\nF3 and B9 have highest availability.\n\nBut F3 gives bonus points (Seat + Prevail wind).\n\nSo **keep F3**, aim to draw third.\n\nThen pair could be T3 or B9.\n\nBut you need to discard one of T3/T4/B9/F3.\n\nDiscard **T4** \u2014 it contributes nothing, cannot form chow, not valuable.\n\nT3 has slightly more potential (could pair, or connect).\n\nB9 is terminal, might help in waits.\n\nF3 is valuable.\n\nSo **discard T4**.\n\nReason: T4 is the least useful tile, unable to form any chow due to T5 being exhausted, and offers no pairing or strategic advantage. Keeping F3 supports potential wind pung (worth extra points), while T3 and B9 retain more flexibility.\n\nAnswer: Play T4"}
